  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="7.1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Duns">Duns</ref> 7.1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="7.1" corresp="#wit-q">Cetere res ungesaþene (id est non uise) possunt iuramento comprobari et secundum hoc reddi. Capra duos d.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="7.1" corresp="#wit-b">Oðre ungesawene ðing man mot mid aðe gewyrðan, ⁊ syððan be ðam gyldan.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#unattributed" corresp="#wit-b">Other unseen things [i.e. not on the above list?] may be valued [by the plaintiff] by [taking an] oath, and paid out accordingly.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Andere, unsichtbare [abstracte] Sachen dürfen [vom Kläger] durch Eid bewerthet und [ihm] dann demgemäss bezahlt werden.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Other, unseen [abstract] things may be valued by oath [by the plaintiff] and then paid [to him] accordingly.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">The Quadripartitus's ‘Capra duos d.’ at the end of this clause belongs to 7 in the OE (see the note there).</note>
      </div>
    </body>
